The model, verified:

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;       /* main axis: left→right (column flips it) */
  justify-content: space-between;  /* ALONG main axis */
  align-items: center;      /* cross axis — the vertical center in row */
  gap: 12px;
  flex-wrap: wrap;          /* allow rows to break */
}

/* THE ITEM TRIO — how free space is shared */
.item {
  flex-grow: 1;     /* take a share of FREE space (default 0) */
  flex-shrink: 1;   /* yield space when overflowing (default 1) */
  flex-basis: 0%;   /* starting size BEFORE grow/shrink (default auto) */
}
/* flex: 1  → 1 1 0%   — EQUAL columns regardless of content
   flex: 1 1 auto → size to CONTENT first, then share the rest
   flex: 0 0 200px → fixed — neither grows nor shrinks */

/* THE CENTERING RECIPE (both axes, two lines) */
.center { display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; }
```

The axis flip (the #1 confusion): justify-content ALWAYS works on the MAIN axis, align-items on the CROSS axis — when flex-direction is column, justify- becomes vertical and align- horizontal. Internalize 'main vs cross', not 'x vs y'.

The shrink mechanics (the overflow interview question): when items overflow the container, flex-shrink divides the DEFICIT proportionally to basis — flex-shrink: 0 pins an item (sidebars that shouldn't compress), min-width: 0 on flex items is the classic fix for content (long words, inputs) refusing to shrink below content size.
